; LIMIT 02: a function cannot take "where the next byte comes from" as an
; argument, so code that consumes bytes is written once per source.
;
; csvcol's parser has to read from a file in the program and from a string in
; its tests. L0 has no function values and no handle over an in-memory
; string, so either the parser is written twice, or it is turned inside out
; into a pure step function (state, byte) -> state that each source drives,
; which is what programs/csvcol does. wordfreq instead writes its tokenizer
; loop twice (count_list in wordfreq.l0, scan in main.l0).
;
; The inside-out parser allocates a state block per byte. Measured on a 14.5
; MB CSV (programs/csvcol/gen.py 200000 8), column 5, programs/measure.sh:
;
;   csvcol, the step function driven by a file         1.07 s   1.6 MB
;   02-...-direct.l0, one function per state, reading   0.27 s   1.5 MB
;   Python's csv module                                0.19 s
;
; Both L0 versions print the same bytes as Python's csv module. Testability
; costs a factor of four.
;
; The smallest program: count newlines from any source. This is what one
; would write, and what tools/l0check says about it:
;
;   02-no-byte-source-argument.l0:38:19: error: from_stdin is a function, and L0 has no function values
;
; A third workaround exists: tests write their input to a temporary file,
; and the parser takes an output IoFile too, so tests read its output back.
; That keeps one fast parser at the price of file plumbing in every test.

(def from_stdin () int
  (read_byte))

(def count_newlines ((next int) (n int)) int
  (let b next
    (if (lt b 0) n (count_newlines next (if (eq b 10) (add n 1) n)))))

(def main () int
  (count_newlines from_stdin 0))
